ding Andrew Foong, Karin Strauss, Keqiang Yan, Cristian Bodnar, Rianne van den Berg, Frank Noé, Marwin Segler, Elise van der Pol, and Max Welling. We are also grateful for useful feedback from the Microsoft Azure Quantum team, including Chi Chen, Leopold Talirz and Nathan Baker. Finally, we thank the AI on Xbox Team for providing part of the compute resources required for this work.\n\n  Line 4082:         member institutions, ,\n\n\n/home/hal/.local/share/opencode/tool-output/tool_14b8f94c5001JflMNJGwwTYNXW:\n  Line 1816: We gratefully acknowledge support from our **major funders**, [**member institutions**](https://info.arxiv.org/about/ourmembers.html), , and all contributors.\n\n\n/home/hal/.local/share/opencode/tool-output/tool_14b9e99f1001JfapqbASwueIGm:\n  Line 129: Because the two members of every pair have the same verdict,\n\n  Line 1967: We gratefully acknowledge support from our **major funders**, [**member institutions**](https://info.arxiv.org/about/ourmembers.html), , and all contributors.\n\n\n/home/hal/.local/share/opencode/tool-output/tool_14be0f4b2001ovCwyPfJLU7F5m:\n  Line 533: We gratefully acknowledge support from our **major funders**, [**member institutions**](https://info.arxiv.org/about/ourmembers.html), , and all contributors.\n\n\n/home/hal/.local/share/opencode/tool-output/tool_14b9a68ba001BMxHuiZFcGnqct:\n  Line 129: Because the two members of every pair have the same verdict,\n\n  Line 1967: We gratefully acknowledge support from our **major funders**, [**member institutions**](https://info.arxiv.org/about/ourmembers.html), , and all contributors.\n\n\n/home/hal/.local/share/opencode/tool-output/tool_14b6863e2001FRoxTCVWrB8wma:\n  Line 844: We gratefully acknowledge support from our **major funders**, [**member institutions**](https://info.arxiv.org/about/ourmembers.html), , and all contributors.\n\n\n/home/hal/.local/share/opencode/tool-output/tool_14bf038f0001VQe1H3chANjAFy:\n  Line 2351:         member institutions, ,\n\n\n/home/hal/.local/share/opencode/tool-output/tool_14bd98e8e00192YR4sclD16to6:\n  Line 1732:         member institutions, ,\n\n\n/home/hal/.local/share/opencode/tool-output/tool_14be0fef1001nugnsaw5dAW2Q3:\n  Line 812: We gratefully acknowledge support from our **major funders**, [**member institutions**](https://info.arxiv.org/about/ourmembers.html), , and all contributors.\n\n\n/home/hal/.local/share/opencode/tool-output/tool_14be81b99001zXqh27CWLeNLh8:\n  Line 812: We gratefully acknowledge support from our **major funders**, [**member institutions**](https://info.arxiv.org/about/ourmembers.html), , and all contributors.\n\n\n/home/hal/.local/share/opencode/tool-output/tool_14ba0ba42001Z7Zu5t2stUBgBf:\n  Line 129: Because the two members of every pair have the same verdict,\n\n  Line 1967: We gratefully acknowledge support from our **major funders**, [**member institutions**](https://info.arxiv.org/about/ourmembers.html), , and all contributors.\n\n\n/home/hal/.local/share/opencode/tool-output/tool_14bd07689001jz31oo21dYT97w:\n  Line 189: The room temperature dataset predominantly consists of NASICON, garnet, perovskite, glass, thio-LISICON, and LISICON type materials, each with more than 27 members. The anion chemistries of the materials are provided in Table [2](/articles/s41524-022-00951-z#Tab2), showing that 75% of the materials in the database are pure oxide compounds (consisting of 44% NASICON, 19% garnet, 18% perovskite, and 8% LISICON type materials), 12% are pure sulfides, and 2% are pure halide compounds. Mixed anion materials (oxyhalides, oxysulphides, etc.) make up 11% of the materials included (46% of these are argyrodites such as Li6PS5Cl, and 16% are antiperovskites such as Li3OCl). In general, materials containing sulfur as an anion exhibit higher minimum and maximum conductivities which is supportive of the outlook that is commonly encountered in the literature that sulfides exhibit the highest Li ion conductivities.\n\n  Line 271: As an aim of machine learning models is to predict the behaviour of as-yet unknown materials, it is important to distinguish between performance in interpolation between materials that have similar chemistries, where similar structure-property-composition relationships would be expected, and in extrapolation to materials characterized by structure and bonding that is not found in the training set. For example, predicting performance within a solid solution family with some members in the training set used would be interpolation, whereas evaluating the conductivity from a material with a new structure type would be extrapolation.\n","metadata":{"matches":53,"truncated":false},"title":"7424|7,424|member|SFNO|HENS","time":{"start":1792285242258,"end":1792285242347}},"id":"prt_14c86735c0011GxM8w1DdydkDH","sessionID":"ses_eb37a4767ffeDEc61JntJTRnH1","messageID":"msg_14c8649cf001xWRS4AaEYpb8rw"}}
